many sci-fi fans have long hoped for a  version of dune that would do greater  visual and narrative justice to frank  herbert's work now director denis  villeneuve has been drafted to bring it  to the big screen here's everything we  know about the 2021 adaptation of doom  so far  the road to making a new cinematic  version of dune was lengthy even before  the current roster of talent signed on  for the film director denis villeneuve  who'd long hoped to helm a new  adaptation of the novel signed on to  make the film in 2017. however it wasn't  until two years later that the project  actually landed a 2020 release date then  the covet 19 pandemic hit making a 2020  release for dune very unlikely and  leaving warner brothers to make some new  moves in october of 2020 warner brothers  announced that the film would shift to  october 1st 2021 then in december the  studio dropped some even bigger news its  entire 2021 film slate including doom  would also simultaneously premiere on  the hbo max streaming service giving  viewers a chance to watch the film at  home without waiting  it's time any dune adaptation faces a  monumental challenge in assembling a  cast to phil herbert's universe it  starts with finding the perfect actors  to stand in the center of it all as the  etredes family in 2018 oscar nominee  timothy chalamet joined the film as paul  atreides making dune his first  blockbuster role shortly after reports  of chalamet's involvement started  circulating rebecca ferguson joined the  film as paul's mother the beni jesuit  woman lady jessica she first begins  teaching him the gifts that will later  manifest as messianic abilities with the  two most prominent members of house of  tradies in place it was time for the  film to cast duke leto atreides this is  paul's father who brings his family to  arrakis to take over leadership of the  planet and finds only trouble in early  2019 it was revealed that duke ledo  would be played by oscar isaac who adds  another major sci-fi franchise to his  resume just a few years after joining  the star wars galaxy these three actors  form the core family unit that is house  of trades but they're far from the only  major stars in the film  dune is an epic novel spanning several  years and featuring two major royal  houses atreides and harkenin who battle  each other for supremacy on the planet  one that also includes its own native  race the fremen that means a massive  cast needed to be assembled to make the  film work because this is a denis  villeneuve film adaptation of one of the  most beloved genre novels ever a-list  actors have lined up to make the  supporting cast for dune a truly  all-star ensemble among the stars  assembled to round out the film's cast  are stellan skarsgard jason momoa javier  bardem zendaya josh brolin david  dasmalchen dave bautista and charlotte  rambling it's an exciting diverse  roundup of some of the most acclaimed  actors working right now in both action  blockbusters and prestige dramas it's  exactly the right blend for herbert's  sprawling space opera of warring  dynasties  dune is one of the most celebrated and  influential science fiction novels ever  written first published in 1965 frank  herbert's novel is set amid a future  civilization built on a kind of  interplanetary feudal system there's a  central government led by an emperor and  various planets are controlled by his  vassals which take the title of baron or  duke the main action of dune takes place  on the planet arrakis it's one of the  most important fiefdoms in the empire  because its vast deserts contain the  spice an addictive and valuable  substance the novel follows the atreides  family as they are given control of  arrakis by the emperor only to find that  a vast plot to overthrow them is already  underway amid these political struggles  duke lido's atreides young son paul  begins to manifest superhuman abilities  that make him a messiah figure among the  local fremont a rebellion begins and  paul's abilities make him into a leader  fit to challenge the emperor himself  hello duke of course herbert's novel is  much more complicated than that and  after dune he just kept going herbert  himself wrote five sequels to the novel  and concluded his series with chapter  house dune in 1985 but the story didn't  end there herbert's son brian picked up  the torch from his father and with  novelist kevin j anderson has published  more than a dozen more dune books many  of them prequels to the original novel  the earliest attempts to adapt dune for  the screen kicked off just a few years  after the novel was published in the  mid-1970s director alejandro jorowski  launched a tremendously ambitious effort  to bring the novel to the screen the  project involved everyone from orson  welles to salvador dali to pink floyd  jodorowsky eventually abandoned the  project when the financing for the film  could not be fully realized but his  efforts were later chronicled in the  2013 documentary dodorowski's doom the  post star wars era improved the fortunes  of dune as a movie so in 1984 a big  screen version helmed by director david  lynch was finally released starring kyle  mclaughlin as paula tradies although it  has since become a cult film doom was a  box office flop and earned negative  reviews lynch not yet the household name  he would be after twin peaks was  frustrated by a lack of creative control  and eventually disowned his dune  altogether a longer and more faithful  adaptation of herbert's novel arrived on  the small screen in 2000 as frank  herbert's dune it's a tv miniseries  written by john harrison that aired in  three two hour installments on the  sci-fi channel it was successful enough  that a sequel frank herbert's children  of dune followed in 2003 and adapted the  next two novels in herbert's series  attempts to bring dune back to the big  screen kicked into gear in 2008 when  paramount's pictures announced peter  berg would direct a new adaptation that  effort eventually stalled and in 2016  legendary pictures announced that they  had landed the film and tv rights for  doom  soon after legendary's announcement that  they would be adapting dune for the  screen again reports began to circulate  that director denivel new was the  studio's choice to direct the new film  version villeneuve's involvement was  confirmed by brian herbert in 2017 after  he dazzled audiences with a rival but  before blade runner 2049 arrived in  theaters to critical acclaim thanks to  those two films in particular villeneuve  is one of the most important voices in  science fiction cinema right now he's a  lifelong dune fan who called the chance  to adapt the book the project of his  life but he made it clear that he  wouldn't be relying on the lynch film  version at all villeneuve said i mean  david lynch is one of the best  filmmakers alive i have massive respect  for him but when i saw his adaptation i  was impressed but it was not what i had  dreamed of so i'm trying to make the  adaptation of my dreams it will not have  any link with the david lynch movie i'm  going back to the book and going to the  images that came out when i read it  frank herbert's original novel is not  the longest genre epic you're likely to  find on the shelves at your local  bookstores but it is among the most  dense dune is a book absolutely packed  with plot covering dozens of characters  multiple groups and cultures and a vast  mythology some of which is only hinted  at in the first novel it's the kind of  book so full of detail that it needs an  appendix at the back so you can look up  various invented terms for more clarity  that means adapting the book to the  screen is hard particularly when you're  working within the constraints of a  big-budget film these days that pretty  much demands you weigh in with a runtime  of three hours or less even with that in  mind though adapting dune as one three  hour story is a daunting prospect that's  defeated filmmakers in the past so for  vilnius film version the solution was  obvious don't try to make dune one film  vilnev made it clear that he was setting  a goal of two films for dune in the  summer of 2018 brian herbert confirmed  that the screenplay for the new film  would adapt approximately half of the  first novel in the series it's not clear  how far approximately half will take us  but the novel does include a time jump  to allow paula tradies to age a bit at  one point so it's possible the first  film will leave off there  brian herbert confirmed in 2018 that  villeneuve's desire to split dune into  two films had come to fruition and that  the film we'd see first would only adapt  half the novel this was reaffirmed in  the spring of 2019 by legendary picture  ceo joshua grodd who added a little  context as to why the split happened he  shared there's a backstory that was  hinted at in some of the books that we  expanded also when you read the book  there's a logical place to stop the  movie before the book is over there's  one really interesting wrinkle in all of  this though yes dune will be split into  two films but it's not like the lord of  the rings peter jackson shot all of  those movies back to back but vilnev is  not making one dune film right after the  other instead legendary seems to be  waiting to see how the first film fares  before launching into production on a  sequel this adds intrigue to what  villeneuve said when he first discussed  his two movie plan he said doom will  probably take two years to make the goal  is to make two films maybe more so far  the only adaptation we've gotten beyond  herbert's first novel is the children of  dune miniseries however if villeneuve  has his way we could one day see a film  version of other books  after years of anticipation the first  official trailer for dune arrived in  september of 2020. this was back when  warner brothers was still holding out  hope that the film might make it into  theaters that december that of course  didn't happen but the trailer still  managed to capture the hearts and  imaginations of fans raising the  anticipation level for the film more  than a year before its eventual release  the first trailer is much more of a  teaser than a full plot breakdown it  spends much of its time with paula  tradies as he grapples with visions of  what might happen to him and his family  as they head for the desert planet of  arrakis we see him tested tormented and  doing his best to be strong even as the  dangers of the planet become apparent  the trailer is of course packed with  dazzling visuals leading up to the  reveal of a sand worm at the end the  second official trailer released in  summer of 2021 digs more heavily into  the plot and the supporting cast of the  film and pays particular attention to  chani the fremen girl paul eventually  falls in love with in this trailer we  get a much better sense of the plot paul  begins to realize not only that his  family is in grave danger from the evil  hearkenings but that he himself might  not actually be the person to lead house  treaties into the future it's a much  fuller view of the film one that allows  us to see more of the internal dynamics  of the ensemble as well as some great  action sequences  get everything with guns off the ground  go  not all were happy when warner media  announced dune would simultaneously  premiere in theaters and on the hbo max  streaming service including dune  director denis villeneuve in a statement  released a variety at the time  villeneuve blasted warner's parent  company at t he explains that he learned  about the release plans quote in the  news like everyone else he wrote with  this decision a t has hijacked one of  the most respectable and important  studios in film history there is  absolutely no love for cinema nor for  the audience here though villeneuve was  not the only critic of the release  strategy he has remained among the  loudest in an august 2021 interview with  total film he called the move ridiculous  and regarding watching dune on a home tv  said it's like driving a speedboat in  your bathtub  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  upcoming movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

